01:01:27,800 --> 01:01:30,760
Because receiver knows the data transfer rate is 1 BPS.

1058
01:01:31,320 --> 01:01:33,039
At the 11th second,

1059
01:01:33,039 --> 01:01:35,019
if you receive the start bit,

1060
01:01:35,380 --> 01:01:38,739
then you should receive stop bit at the 20th second.

1061
01:01:38,739 --> 01:01:40,800
because you know that it allows a rate.

1062
01:01:41,300 --> 01:01:44,019
And at the 20th second, if you receive value 1,

1063
01:01:44,840 --> 01:01:46,760
now that means there is a start bit.

1064
01:01:47,440 --> 01:01:48,920
Sorry, stop bit, right?

1065
01:01:49,719 --> 01:01:52,119
If you receive value 1, that means it's a stop bit.

1066
01:01:52,460 --> 01:01:56,059
If you can recognize the stop bit and the start bit in between,

1067
01:01:57,280 --> 01:01:59,619
you can have other 8 bits.

1068
01:01:59,619 --> 01:02:00,920
You can extract them.

1069
01:02:01,599 --> 01:02:05,159
Those extracted 8 bits, you can compare with the ASCII table

1070
01:02:05,159 --> 01:02:06,559
and recognize the character.

1071
01:02:06,559 --> 01:02:14,260
So this is how we do a synchronous data transfer using serial communication.

1072
01:02:14,599 --> 01:02:16,639
One bit at a time we send.

1073
01:02:17,280 --> 01:02:18,639
We send the start bit.

1074
01:02:19,360 --> 01:02:20,880
Then we send the LSB.

1075
01:02:21,300 --> 01:02:24,699
Then we send the other bits of the character.

1076
01:02:25,619 --> 01:02:27,179
And then we send the MSB.

1077
01:02:27,420 --> 01:02:28,579
Then we send the stop bit.

1078
01:02:28,980 --> 01:02:30,300
One frame we send.

1079
01:02:30,300 --> 01:02:36,519
Using the start bit and the stop bit, the receiver can recognize the frame.

1080
01:02:36,559 --> 01:02:43,039
and from the frame between start bit and stop bit there are 8 characters, 8 bits.

1081
01:02:44,159 --> 01:02:50,739
The receiver can extract those 8 bits and those 8 bits according to SP table receiver can recognize.

1082
01:02:50,739 --> 01:02:56,579
If receiver receives 0 at the beginning, receiver knows that is the start bit.

1083
01:02:57,059 --> 01:03:01,599
And from that point of time, receiver starts counting.

1084
01:03:02,019 --> 01:03:05,019
Every second 1 bit, every second 1 bit.

1085
01:03:05,019 --> 01:03:11,920
And the receiver expects at the 10th second to receive value 1, stop bit.

1086
01:03:12,460 --> 01:03:17,199
If it could receive value 1, that means that is the stop bit.

1087
01:03:17,619 --> 01:03:21,440
So receiver can recognize stop bit and start bit.

1088
01:03:22,260 --> 01:03:26,440
And in between 8 bits, receiver can extract the 8 bits.

1089
01:03:26,679 --> 01:03:29,320
According to ASCII table, it can recognize the character.

1090
01:03:30,179 --> 01:03:32,400
Then if receiver receives another zero,

1091
01:03:33,039 --> 01:03:37,340
the receiver recognizes that as the start bit of the second character.

1092
01:03:37,659 --> 01:03:39,159
Then receiver starts counting.

1093
01:03:40,099 --> 01:03:41,860
And at the 20th second,

1094
01:03:42,500 --> 01:03:46,980
receiver expects to receive another value 1.

1095
01:03:47,739 --> 01:03:48,840
That is the stop bit.

1096
01:03:48,840 --> 01:03:53,059
If receiver could receive value 1 at the 20th second,

1097
01:03:53,480 --> 01:03:54,900
that's the stop bit.

1098
01:03:55,300 --> 01:03:59,300
So if receiver could recognize the stop bit and start bit,

1099
01:03:59,320 --> 01:04:01,739
In between them there are 8 bits.

1100
01:04:01,860 --> 01:04:03,340
So receiver can extract.

1101
01:04:03,920 --> 01:04:10,659
And those 8 bits receiver can compare with the ASCII table and recognize character B.

1102
01:04:11,000 --> 01:04:13,780
This is how the communication happens.

1103
01:04:14,079 --> 01:04:14,840
Can you understand?

1104
01:04:15,300 --> 01:04:21,440
To do this receiver must know data transfer speed of the transmitter.

1105
01:04:21,840 --> 01:04:27,280
That means receiver and transmitter must agree on data transfer.

1106
01:04:27,280 --> 01:04:40,780
Now in case transmitter is slower than the agreed data transfer rate, receiver expects at the 10th second to receive stop beat, that is value 1.

1107
01:04:41,639 --> 01:04:46,559
Receiver expects to receive value 1 at the 10th second.

1108
01:04:46,960 --> 01:04:51,079
But transmitter is slower than the agreed data transfer rate.

1109
01:04:51,079 --> 01:04:55,599
So will receiver receive value 1 at the 10th second?

1110
01:04:56,219 --> 01:04:56,920
Don't know.

1111
01:04:57,280 --> 01:05:01,519
If receiver could receive value 1 at the 10th second,

1112
01:05:02,260 --> 01:05:04,780
receiver will think it is the stop bit.

1113
01:05:05,099 --> 01:05:07,219
But actually it is not the stop bit.

1114
01:05:07,420 --> 01:05:07,619
Why?

1115
01:05:07,960 --> 01:05:09,099
Transmitter is slow.

1116
01:05:09,099 --> 01:05:13,280
So receiver will recognize a wrong character.

1117
01:05:13,699 --> 01:05:17,460
In case if receiver receives 0 at the 10th second,

1118
01:05:18,119 --> 01:05:23,440
receiver expects to receive 1 but it receives 0 at the 10th second,

1119
01:05:23,760 --> 01:05:25,500
receiver will go mad.

1120
01:05:25,500 --> 01:05:30,460
And receiver will recognize some weird character.

1121
01:05:31,059 --> 01:05:34,260
That's what happens when you use Arduino.

1122
01:05:34,900 --> 01:05:40,500
If the data transfer rate, if the board rate, if you couldn't set properly,

1123
01:05:40,659 --> 01:05:44,719
you get the serial monitor, some weird characters printed.

1124
01:05:45,179 --> 01:05:46,400
This is the situation.

1125
01:05:46,980 --> 01:05:52,539
Because receiver expects, if the first second zero, that is start bit,

1126
01:05:52,539 --> 01:05:54,820
10th second I should get

1127
01:05:54,820 --> 01:05:55,920
1 stop it.

1128
01:05:56,840 --> 01:05:58,780
Receiver expect to receive 1

1129
01:05:58,780 --> 01:06:00,780
at the 10th second. If you got 1

1130
01:06:00,780 --> 01:06:02,539
at the 10th second you think

1131
01:06:02,539 --> 01:06:04,760
it is the stop it. But if

1132
01:06:04,760 --> 01:06:06,579
you receive 0 at the 10th second

1133
01:06:06,579 --> 01:06:08,559
then you will go mad. If the

1134
01:06:08,559 --> 01:06:10,460
transmitter transfers

1135
01:06:10,460 --> 01:06:12,840
data at the agreed rate of

1136
01:06:12,840 --> 01:06:14,340
data transfer rate

1137
01:06:14,340 --> 01:06:16,679
then it's alright. If transmitter

1138
01:06:16,679 --> 01:06:18,119
is slower than

1139
01:06:18,119 --> 01:06:20,860
the receiver. Let's say 10%

1140
01:06:20,860 --> 01:06:22,199
slower than the receiver.

1141
01:06:22,539 --> 01:06:29,139
Then at the 10th second, receiver will receive not the stop bit, but the MSB of the first character.

1142
01:06:29,500 --> 01:06:32,519
Because transmitter is 10% lower than the receiver.

1143
01:06:32,519 --> 01:06:40,619
So at the 10th second, receiver will receive not the stop bit, but the MSB of the first character.

1144
01:06:40,980 --> 01:06:46,860
If the MSB of the first character is 1, receiver will think it is the stop bit.

1145
01:06:47,280 --> 01:06:50,739
And it will recognize the wrong character.

1146
01:06:50,739 --> 01:06:56,280
If the MSB is zero, receiver will go mad.

1147
01:06:56,460 --> 01:06:56,639
Why?

1148
01:06:56,760 --> 01:07:02,039
Receiver expect at the 10th second to receive one, but it got zero.

1149
01:07:02,280 --> 01:07:03,199
It will go mad.

1150
01:07:03,559 --> 01:07:04,679
Can you understand?

1151
01:07:05,000 --> 01:07:05,179
Is it?

1152
01:07:05,659 --> 01:07:06,320
Yes, sir.

